WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.674
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Thursday, November 19.

2
00:00:09.674 --> 00:00:21.658
That first story matters because the first-ever U.S.-China-Russia trilateral summit produced a four-point declaration on nuclear risk and the Korean Peninsula was not in it.

3
00:00:21.658 --> 00:00:28.170
South Korea also crossed one trillion dollars in annual exports today, at 2:09 this afternoon.

4
00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:39.950
First, the Shenzhen summit and what it left out. Donald Trump, Xi Jinping, and Vladimir Putin met at the Kylin Villa in Shenzhen, on the sidelines of the APEC summit.

5
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:46.260
It was the first-ever U.S.-China-Russia trilateral, and it produced a four-point joint declaration.

6
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:59.581
The three leaders pledged dialogue on reducing nuclear risks and preventing proliferation, reforming the United Nations, increasing production and supply of refined petroleum, and respecting state sovereignty.

7
00:00:59.581 --> 00:01:12.137
North Korea was not mentioned. The North Korean nuclear program was not mentioned. Denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula was not mentioned. South Korean and Chinese analysts flagged the absence.

8
00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:21.315
Kyunghyang Shinmun reported that the three powers' divergent nuclear calculations on the Korean Peninsula produced only a gap in the communique.

9
00:01:21.315 --> 00:01:39.416
The CSIS Korea Chair's Capital Cable podcast argues that North Korea's nuclear program was conspicuously absent from the declaration, and examines what the trilateral's framing of nuclear risk reduction, without naming the Korean Peninsula, means for North Korea's strategic calculus.

10
00:01:39.416 --> 00:01:50.060
It connects Kim Jong Un's simultaneous east-coast military inspection to the unresolved question of a Trump-Kim meeting. On Capitol Hill, VOA Korean reported that U.S.

11
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:59.238
lawmakers hold divergent priorities as the summit convened, with congressional attention spanning Iran and North Korea alongside trade concerns.

12
00:01:59.238 --> 00:02:07.333
Also at APEC: President Lee Jae-myung and Putin held a 10-minute meeting at the welcome banquet, the first Lee-Putin encounter.

13
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:19.379
A senior Blue House official said both leaders agreed on the need to prevent further escalation on the Korean Peninsula and agreed to pursue bilateral cooperation in areas free of friction.

14
00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:31.170
The bottom line from Shenzhen: the first great-power trilateral of this era addressed nuclear risk in the abstract, and left the Korean Peninsula's specific nuclear problem unaddressed.

15
00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.972
Second, the International Atomic Energy Agency's confirmation on Yongbyon.

16
00:02:36.972 --> 00:02:51.212
The International Atomic Energy Agency Director General Rafael Grossi told the Board of Governors in Vienna that North Korea has completed external construction of a new annex to the original Yongbyon uranium enrichment facility.

17
00:02:51.212 --> 00:02:58.115
Construction finished since mid-September, and the structure appeared externally complete by late October. N.K.

18
00:02:58.115 --> 00:03:05.453
News reports the annex is similar in size to one built on the north side of the same Yongbyon building five years ago.

19
00:03:05.453 --> 00:03:11.982
State media in January 2025 appeared to show new centrifuge cascades filling a comparable annex interior.

20
00:03:11.982 --> 00:03:22.740
Grossi also confirmed that the 5 megawatt electric reactor is continuing its seventh irradiation cycle, and that the light water reactor has been operating since late April.

21
00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:35.488
That is three simultaneous lines of nuclear activity at Yongbyon: a newly completed enrichment annex, an operating research reactor in its seventh cycle, and a light water reactor running since the spring.

22
00:03:35.488 --> 00:03:45.624
The same Board of Governors meeting in Vienna also took up a separate International Atomic Energy Agency report on South Korea's nuclear-powered submarine program.

23
00:03:45.624 --> 00:03:56.569
The agency expressed satisfaction with Seoul's cooperation on safeguards and affirmed that all nuclear materials in South Korea have been used for peaceful purposes since 2007.

24
00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:05.710
Watch what Grossi says publicly after the Board session closes, and whether any member state calls for a formal response to the Yongbyon expansion.

25
00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:17.241
Third, South Korea's Supreme Court ruling and the political fallout. The Supreme Court partially overturned ex-Prime Minister Han Duck-soo's 15-year prison sentence.

26
00:04:17.241 --> 00:04:30.953
Han had been convicted for his role in former President Yoon Suk Yeol's December 2024 martial law bid. The court found that Han's proposal to convene a Cabinet meeting did not meet the threshold for insurrection intent.

27
00:04:30.953 --> 00:04:37.089
His case has been remanded to the Seoul High Court. The Democratic Party's response was immediate.

28
00:04:37.089 --> 00:04:47.858
Party leader Kim Min-seok announced the party would pursue impeachment of Supreme Court Chief Justice Cho Hee-dae, calling the ruling a, quote, "historical act of treason."

29
00:04:47.858 --> 00:04:51.928
The People Power Party leader Jang Dong-hyuk welcomed the ruling.

30
00:04:51.928 --> 00:05:03.073
Jang called it, quote, "the judiciary's rebuke of the Lee administration's insurrection narrative," and said, quote, "another fabricated indictment has revealed its true nature."

31
00:05:03.073 --> 00:05:12.840
The People Power Party had also welcomed a November 18 acquittal of former Daegu Mayor Choo Kyung-ho on charges related to the December 2024 emergency vote.

32
00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:24.048
In the National Assembly, the Democratic Party brought the Democratic Merit Act, which concerns recognition and benefits for participants in pro-democracy movements, to the floor.

33
00:05:24.048 --> 00:05:33.940
The People Power Party launched a filibuster in opposition. The confirmation hearing for Justice Minister nominee Lee Yong-cheol has been set for November 24.

34
00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:42.581
The civic group Peoples' Solidarity for Participatory Democracy submitted 26 policy questions to the nominee on judicial reform direction.

35
00:05:42.581 --> 00:05:53.350
A Gallup Korea poll taken November 3 to 5 put presidential approval at 41 percent, with the Democratic Party at 36 percent support and the People Power Party at 28 percent.

36
00:05:54.550 --> 00:06:06.197
Now to Pyongyang. State media led today with two stories. First, KCNA reported that Russia will erect a monument this year honoring North Korean troops who fought in the Kursk region.

37
00:06:06.197 --> 00:06:14.343
This is the first time North Korean state media has acknowledged North Korean military participation in the conflict in Ukraine.

38
00:06:14.343 --> 00:06:29.809
Second, KCNA announced North Korea will convene a meeting of cell-level and primary party organization leaders of the Korean Workers' Party this month, a routine mobilization mechanism that often precedes major political or economic campaigns.

39
00:06:29.809 --> 00:06:38.910
Kim Jong Un himself inspected the Kim Jong Suk Naval University and construction sites in Riwon and Ragwon counties of South Hamgyong Province.

40
00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:52.593
At the university, Kim said, quote, "The university's primary duty is to train professional naval commanders." He criticized what he called the naval activities of wicked enemies. State media made no mention of U.S.

41
00:06:52.593 --> 00:07:05.767
talks. The bottom line: Pyongyang's first acknowledgment of its troops fighting in Ukraine comes as Kim's foreign affairs secretary, Jo Yong Won, returns from Moscow, where he met President Putin. Daily N.K.

42
00:07:05.767 --> 00:07:11.750
reports that North Korean authorities issued a gag order on domestic discussion of that visit.

43
00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:14.828
The rest of the news, quickly.

44
00:07:14.828 --> 00:07:28.290
South Korea crossed one trillion dollars in annual exports at 2:09 this afternoon, the fourth country globally to do so, after Germany, the United States, and China, with AI-related semiconductors driving the surge.

45
00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:37.432
President Lee, posting from Shenzhen as he departed the APEC summit, noted that South Korea's exports stood at 10 billion dollars fifty years ago.

46
00:07:37.432 --> 00:07:50.267
The Korea-China free trade agreement services and investment follow-on talks concluded after 8 years and 8 months. Presidents Lee and Xi declared substantive conclusion at their APEC bilateral in Shenzhen.

47
00:07:50.267 --> 00:08:01.475
The two leaders agreed to accelerate market access, expand cultural exchanges, and send a pair of pandas to South Korea ahead of the 35th anniversary of diplomatic ties next year.

48
00:08:01.475 --> 00:08:11.243
Korean entertainment, gaming, and travel stocks surged on the news, with drama producer Studio Dragon up nearly 30 percent. The U.S. abstained from the U.N.

49
00:08:11.243 --> 00:08:16.690
Third Committee resolution on North Korean human rights for the first time in 21 years.

50
00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:27.084
Washington called for a vote, departing from the established consensus practice, then abstained, citing objections to a reference to the International Criminal Court.

51
00:08:27.084 --> 00:08:36.914
The resolution passed 95 to 14, with 58 abstentions, co-sponsored by 58 countries including South Korea. It goes to the General Assembly plenary in December.

52
00:08:36.914 --> 00:08:50.063
The European Union created a dedicated EU Sanctions Office to coordinate restrictions against Russia and North Korea, combining sanctions expertise from policy design through enforcement and anti-circumvention.

53
00:08:50.063 --> 00:09:01.208
38 North reports North Korea showcased a domestic artificial intelligence chatbot at a recent national I.T. exhibition, the first public debut of a North Korea-developed AI tool.

54
00:09:01.208 --> 00:09:20.305
Also in analysis worth your attention: Lee Byong-chul, writing in 38 North, argues that Seoul should pursue a formal two-state framework with Pyongyang, a managed separation that institutionalizes division as sovereign equals rather than maintaining unification frameworks neither side can operationalize.

55
00:09:20.305 --> 00:09:34.330
And The Diplomat profiles Newtype, a South Korean defense startup whose CEO says cooperation with Ukraine has continued, noting that a Russian strike on its system suggested Moscow had identified and targeted the technology.

56
00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:44.550
Russian military aircraft entered South Korea's air defense zone for a second consecutive day. The South Korean Air Force scrambled fighters in response.

57
00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:57.502
A Seoul National University student with a perfect score on the law school entrance exam was found dead amid an artificial intelligence cheating probe. A note cited exhaustion, and police are investigating.

58
00:09:57.502 --> 00:10:08.442
South Korea joined the United States, Australia, and other countries in releasing a statement of principles for a Southeast and East Asia-Pacific scam response contact group.

59
00:10:08.442 --> 00:10:13.660
Seoul plans a Korea-ASEAN scam response cooperation program starting in early 2027.

60
00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:27.159
On Capitol Hill. Incoming House member Dan Koh addressed North Korean citizens directly, saying, quote, "I am thinking of you," and pledged to work to keep the U.S.-Korea alliance strong.

61
00:10:27.159 --> 00:10:39.590
VOA Korean also reported that U.S. lawmakers hold divergent views on priorities as the trilateral summit convened, with congressional attention on both Iran and North Korea alongside trade.

62
00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:51.178
Beyond the region. South Korea joined Canada and other NATO partners as funders of the new NATO STARLIFT initiative to boost alliance launch capabilities in space.

63
00:10:51.178 --> 00:11:09.023
India and South Korea announced 25 specific cooperation outcomes and elevated their bilateral relationship to a, quote, "futuristic partnership," covering defense, the digital economy, and supply chains, following a bilateral summit on the sidelines of the ASEAN summit in Manila.

64
00:11:09.023 --> 00:11:24.510
South Korea and the International Energy Agency jointly launched the Rise Asia clean energy cooperation initiative at the 31st Conference of the Parties in Antalya, Turkey, focusing on clean electrification and energy risk information sharing.

65
00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:38.566
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed at about 5,138, up about 1.4 percent. The won strengthened slightly, trading at about 1,328 to the dollar.

66
00:11:38.566 --> 00:11:53.066
Brent crude fell about 3 percent, to around 89 dollars a barrel. The Bank of Korea's policy rate stands at 3 percent, last set in October. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Thursday, November 19.

67
00:11:53.066 --> 00:11:57.850
The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
